Save 4 MB of main RAM in a DS state, not 16 MB

NDS::DoSavestate wrote main RAM at MainRAMMaxSize, the DSi's 16 MB, whatever the
console. A DS has 4 MB and every access in DS mode is masked to it, so 12 MB of
every DS state were bytes nothing ever reads: a state was about 19 MB where 7 MB
say everything. That size is paid on every save slot written and read, on every
iCloud upload of one, and by the rewind: measured with Tools/nds-bench, a
snapshot of SoulSilver drops from 18.8 to 6.8 MB and from 3.8 to 3.0 ms (the
rest of a snapshot's cost is not the RAM copy).

The format changes, so the minor version goes 0 -> 1:
- A 13.0 state (every state written before this patch) still reads 16 MB and
  loads exactly as before. Checked with Tools/nds-bench on New Super Mario Bros.
  and Pokemon SoulSilver states made on a phone: the patched core loading them
  produces the same pictures and sound, frame for frame, as the unpatched core.
- A 13.1 DS state writes and reads 4 MB. A DSi state keeps its 16 MB.
- An older build given a 13.1 state refuses it ("state from the future"), which
  is WHY this is a version and not a flag: without the bump, an older Retro Pal
  on another device (iCloud syncs save slots) would read 4 MB of RAM and then
  take the following sections for the rest of its 16 MB.

On a future submodule bump, upstream may take minor 1 for something else. This
patch then conflicts on the SAVESTATE_MINOR line and the build stops, on
purpose: our states and upstream's must be re-numbered by hand, never merged by
accident.

Applied at build time by Vendor/melonds-ios/build.sh, after 0001. Specific to
Retro Pal; not an upstream candidate as is (upstream frontends may load states
from other builds).

diff --git a/src/NDS.cpp b/src/NDS.cpp
index f45051e..49453bd 100644
--- a/src/NDS.cpp
+++ b/src/NDS.cpp
@@ -643,7 +643,16 @@ bool NDS::DoSavestate(Savestate* file)
         }
     }
 
-    file->VarArray(MainRAM, MainRAMMaxSize);
+    // A DS has 4 MB of main RAM; MainRAMMaxSize is the DSi's 16 MB, and every
+    // access in DS mode is masked to 4 MB (MainRAMMask), so the other 12 MB of a
+    // DS state were never read by anything. States up to 13.0 carry all 16 MB
+    // and still load exactly as before. From 13.1 a DS writes and reads its 4 MB
+    // only: a state drops from about 19 MB to about 7 MB, and a rewind snapshot,
+    // taken in the middle of a frame, gets about a fifth cheaper.
+    u32 mainRAMSize = MainRAMMaxSize;
+    if (ConsoleType == 0 && file->IsAtLeastVersion(13, 1))
+        mainRAMSize = 0x400000;
+    file->VarArray(MainRAM, mainRAMSize);
     file->VarArray(SharedWRAM, SharedWRAMSize);
     file->VarArray(ARM7WRAM, ARM7WRAMSize);
 
diff --git a/src/Savestate.h b/src/Savestate.h
index f046e43..b705a0c 100644
--- a/src/Savestate.h
+++ b/src/Savestate.h
@@ -25,7 +25,10 @@
 #include "types.h"
 
 #define SAVESTATE_MAJOR 13
-#define SAVESTATE_MINOR 0
+// Retro Pal: 1 = a DS (not a DSi) saves 4 MB of main RAM instead of 16 MB. See
+// NDS::DoSavestate. Only a version bump makes an older build REFUSE such a state
+// ("state from the future") instead of reading 12 MB of the wrong sections.
+#define SAVESTATE_MINOR 1
 
 // bitmask for the savestate config word
 enum
